# Normalise stored line endings so that a diff never turns into a whole-file
# rewrite because two contributors are on different platforms.
* text=auto

# Shell scripts must be checked out with LF on every platform. A CRLF line
# ending after the shebang is part of the interpreter path, so a script checked
# out with CRLF on Windows fails to run at all. This is correctness, not style.
*.sh text eol=lf

# uv writes the lockfile with LF on every platform. Without this, a Windows
# checkout receives CRLF and the next `uv lock` rewrites the whole file, which
# turns a no-op into a diff.
uv.lock text eol=lf

# A workflow's `run:` blocks are shell scripts that a Linux runner executes, and
# a CRLF line ending inside one becomes a literal carriage return in the command
# — the same failure `*.sh` above is protected from, in a file that does not look
# like a script. `text=auto` already normalises what is stored; this pins what is
# checked out, so a runner cannot receive CRLF whatever the checkout did.
.github/workflows/*.yml text eol=lf
.github/workflows/*.yaml text eol=lf

# The chart hashes its own rendered configuration into a pod annotation
# (`inferops.io/configuration-checksum`), so the bytes of every template and
# values file are part of the output. A CRLF checkout renders a different
# checksum from an LF one, and the committed renders under ci/rendered/ can
# only match one of them. Pinning the checkout makes the render the same on
# every platform: before this, the committed renders carried checksums derived
# from a Windows working tree, and the first hosted Linux run failed both.
charts/** text eol=lf

# The proof dashboard is generated from the claim and evidence register and
# compared with what `python -m tools.proof_dashboard --page` prints, and the
# generator writes LF on every platform so that the same register produces the
# same bytes everywhere. Pinning the checkout keeps a Windows working tree from
# acquiring CRLF that the next `--write` would immediately undo.
docs/proof/dashboard.md text eol=lf

# The generated Prometheus rule files are compared byte for byte against what
# `python -m tools.inference_alerts --rules` prints, and a collector's promtool
# reads them out of a Linux container. `text=auto` already normalises what is
# stored; this pins what is checked out, so neither comparison depends on which
# platform wrote the file.
deploy/prometheus/*.yaml text eol=lf
